1
00:03:20,689 --> 00:03:26,689
Make room for new emotions.

2
00:06:41,379 --> 00:06:47,379
Inside Out 2 is a animation, adventure, comedy film directed by Kelsey Mann.

3
00:10:02,068 --> 00:10:08,068
Teenager Riley's mind headquarters is undergoing a sudden demolition to
make room for something entirely unexpected: new Emotions!

4
00:13:22,758 --> 00:13:28,758
Joy, Sadness, Anger, Fear and Disgust, who’ve long been running a successful
operation by all accounts, aren’t sure how to feel when Anxiety shows up.

5
00:16:43,448 --> 00:16:49,448
And it looks like she’s not alone.

6
00:20:04,137 --> 00:20:10,137
Joy (voice), played by Amy Poehler.

7
00:23:24,827 --> 00:23:30,827
Anxiety (voice), played by Maya Hawke.

8
00:26:45,517 --> 00:26:51,517
Riley (voice), played by Kensington Tallman.

9
00:30:06,206 --> 00:30:12,206
Disgust (voice), played by Liza Lapira.

10
00:33:26,896 --> 00:33:32,896
Fear (voice), played by Tony Hale.

11
00:36:47,586 --> 00:36:53,586
Anger (voice), played by Lewis Black.

12
00:40:08,275 --> 00:40:14,275
Inside Out 2 is a 2024 American animated coming-of-age film produced
by Pixar Animation Studios for Walt Disney Pictures.

13
00:43:28,965 --> 00:43:34,965
The sequel to Inside Out (2015), it was directed by Kelsey Mann in his feature film directorial debut and was produced by Mark
Nielsen, from a screenplay written by Meg LeFauve and Dave Holstein, and a story conceived by Mann and LeFauve.

14
00:46:49,655 --> 00:46:55,655
Amy Poehler, Phyllis Smith, Lewis Black, Diane Lane, and Kyle MacLachlan reprise their roles from the first film, with Maya Hawke, Kensington Tallman (replacing Kaitlyn Dias from the first film), Liza Lapira (replacing
Mindy Kaling from the first film), Tony Hale (replacing Bill Hader from the first film), Ayo Edebiri, Lilimar, Grace Lu, Sumayyah Nuriddin-Green, Adèle Exarchopoulos, and Paul Walter Hauser joining the cast.

15
00:50:10,344 --> 00:50:16,344
The film follows Riley's emotions unexpectedly joined by new
emotions, eager to take control of her mind.

16
00:53:31,034 --> 00:53:37,034
Development on Inside Out 2 began in early 2020, with Mann drawing
inspiration from personal childhood experiences.

17
00:56:51,724 --> 00:56:57,724
The creative team initially explored a wider range of new emotions before narrowing the
focus for narrative clarity, with Anxiety emerging as a central addition.

18
01:00:12,413 --> 01:00:18,413
Clinical psychologists, including Lisa Damour and Dacher Keltner, were consulted to ensure an accurate portrayal of
adolescent emotional development, while a group of teenagers provided feedback on character and story authenticity.

19
01:03:33,103 --> 01:03:39,103
The film's premise shifted during development from a
talent show to Riley's involvement in hockey.

20
01:06:53,793 --> 01:06:59,793
The production also marked the first Pixar feature
scored by a woman, Andrea Datzman.

21
01:10:14,482 --> 01:10:20,482
Animation development emphasized spatial consistency through isometric mapping, and casting changes were driven
in part by compensation disputes, resulting in the recasting of the characters Fear and Disgust.

22
01:13:35,172 --> 01:13:41,172
Inside Out 2 premiered at the El Capitan Theatre in Hollywood, Los Angeles, on
June 10, 2024, and was released in the United States on June 14.

23
01:16:55,862 --> 01:17:01,862
The film received positive reviews from critics and was a massive box office success, grossing $1.699 billion worldwide, breaking multiple box-office records, becoming the
highest-grossing film by Pixar and the highest-grossing animated film of all time, though was later overtaken by Ne Zha 2 (2025) in just nine months after its initial release.

24
01:20:16,551 --> 01:20:22,551
It also became the highest-grossing film of 2024 and the
eighth-highest-grossing film at the time of its release.

25
01:23:37,241 --> 01:23:43,241
The film received nominations for Best Animated Feature at the Golden
Globes, Critics' Choice, BAFTAs and Academy Awards.

26
01:26:57,931 --> 01:27:03,931
It additionally received a nomination for Cinematic and Box
Office Achievement at the Golden Globes.

27
01:30:18,620 --> 01:30:24,620
The film explores themes of sadness, disgust,
sequel, computer animation, teenage girl.

28
01:33:39,310 --> 01:33:45,310
The tone is lighthearted.
